{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 830 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d2d098f279390a6b96ad77af6583ce739ddc8bfb4e2153669cd8dc8684e2fea3" :edb/db-before-t 829 :edb/db-after-t 830 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534142 50 #inst "2026-10-06T04:26:54.954Z" 13194139534142 true] [17592186110513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278486 13194139534142 true] [17592186110513 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278493 13194139534142 true] [17592186110514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186278500 13194139534142 true] [17592186111293 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534142 true] [17592186111293 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arctan x$?\n\n1. $(1, \\frac{π}{6})$\n2. $(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{π}{3})$\n3. $(-1,-\\frac{π}{4})$" 13194139534142 false] [17592186111293 1100 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" 13194139534142 true] [17592186111293 1145 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139534142 true] [17592186111295 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534142 true] [17592186111295 1100 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y = \\arctan x$?" 13194139534142 false] [17592186111295 1100 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" 13194139534142 true] [17592186111295 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{1}{4}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139534142 true] [17592186278486 1017 #edb/ref 17592186278487 13194139534142 true] [17592186278486 1089 "q-117327" 13194139534142 true] [17592186278486 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534142 true] [17592186278486 1100 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $\\frac{16\\pi }{3}$\n- $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n- $\\frac{1}{100}$" 13194139534142 true] [17592186278486 1113 #uuid "448d3f01-61bd-5184-a3e1-3f962f3e8d1c" 13194139534142 true] [17592186278486 1145 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/ass